  <h2>The Science of Recovery: Why Breaks Matter More Than You Think</h2>

  <p>Research by psychologists K. Anders Ericsson and others on deliberate practice reveals that elite performers in any domain — musicians, athletes, chess players — do not work longer hours than their peers. They work with greater intensity during shorter periods and recover more completely between sessions. The Pomodoro Technique operationalises this finding for knowledge workers.</p>

  <p>During a five-minute break, your brain consolidates what you have just learned, clears metabolic waste products from neural tissue, and restores glucose levels in the prefrontal cortex — the region responsible for executive function. Skipping breaks does not make you more productive; it makes you stupider, literally. Studies show that cognitive performance degrades measurably after 50–60 minutes of continuous focused work without rest.</p>

  <p>The five-minute break is not a concession to weakness. It is a performance requirement.</p>

  <h2>Common Mistakes</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Skipping breaks:</strong> "I am in the zone, I will keep going." This leads to burnout and diminishing returns. Take the break.</li>
    <li><strong>Multitasking within a Pomodoro:</strong> Each Pomodoro should have one task. Switching tasks mid-cycle defeats the purpose.</li>
    <li><strong>Being too rigid:</strong> If a colleague genuinely needs you, respond. Pomodoro is a tool, not a prison.</li>
    <li><strong>Tracking Pomodoros obsessively:</strong> The count is feedback, not a score. Do not judge your day by Pomodoro count alone.</li>
  </ul>

